(window.FAIAS_IRQ=window.FAIAS_IRQ||{})["IRQ-GOV-012"]={"canvas":[{"n":1,"title":"IRQ ID & Title","html":"<p><strong>IRQ-GOV-012: Effective Operating Envelope Has Drifted from the Approved Envelope Without Re-Assurance</strong></p>\n<p>Register question: \"Has the system's effective operating envelope drifted from its approved envelope — through changes in intended use, user behaviour, prompts, tools, integrations, or the surrounding business process — while the model itself remained unchanged?\"</p>"},{"n":2,"title":"Business Activities Affected","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Every AI system approved against a stated, bounded operating envelope:</strong> lending triage tools quietly becoming lending decision tools; customer-service assistants originally scoped to FAQ answering being asked to draft binding commitments; AML investigation copilots originally scoped to English-language, domestic typologies being applied to cross-border, multilingual cases without re-validation.</li>\n<li><strong>Agentic and tool-integrated systems specifically:</strong> integration drift (a standalone triage tool wired into downstream pricing or payment systems after go-live) and tool/permission expansion are the highest-velocity envelope-drift vectors, because they can occur through a single engineering change with no formal governance touchpoint.</li>\n<li><strong>Any deployment where business teams control prompts or configuration:</strong> prompt drift (\"be more permissive,\" \"handle vulnerable customers directly\") can silently expand the effective envelope with no code change and, absent controls, no governance visibility at all.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":3,"title":"AI Behaviour(s)","html":"<ul>\n<li>N/A directly at the model-behaviour level — the model itself is, by the terms of this IRQ, unchanged; what changes is the <em>use</em> made of it: the tasks assigned, the users granted access, the prompts issued, the tools connected, and the business process surrounding its output. The system faithfully executes whatever it is asked to do within its technical capability, which is precisely why expanding what it is asked to do is invisible to any model-level control.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":4,"title":"Failure Mode","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>The system's effective, in-practice operating envelope — defined by actual intended use, user population, prompt content, connected tools/integrations, and surrounding business process — has expanded beyond the envelope explicitly approved at deployment, without triggering re-assurance, even though the model's own weights and version remain unchanged. Because assurance evidence (validation, testing, risk appetite sign-off) was obtained for the <em>approved</em> envelope, none of it demonstrably applies to the <em>actual</em> one, and no single control owner is positioned to notice the divergence because each individual change (a prompt edit, a new integration, a shift in who uses the tool) can look locally reasonable.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":5,"title":"Potential Consequences","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Customer:</strong> A system validated as a low-risk \"triage tool with 10% human escalation\" that has drifted, through incremental process change, into a de facto \"decision tool with rubber-stamp review\" exposes customers to decisions made with a level of AI autonomy that was never actually tested or approved — the harm is not a new model failure, but an old, untested risk surface now bearing real weight.</li>\n<li><strong>Institution:</strong> The firm's approval paper, safety case and risk appetite sign-off for the system are no longer an accurate description of what is actually happening; in the event of an incident, \"we tested and approved this\" is not a valid defence because what was tested is not what occurred — this is a fundamental integrity failure of the assurance record itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulator/market:</strong> Regulators increasingly expect firms to demonstrate that approved scope and actual use remain reconciled over time (EU AI Act Art. 9's continuous risk-management-system requirement, PRA SS1/23's ongoing-monitoring principle); an envelope-drift finding is a governance-integrity failure distinct from — and often a root cause behind — any specific technical or customer-harm finding.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":6,"title":"Failure Chain","html":"<pre><code>System approved against an explicit, documented operating envelope (designed-for\nscope: task type, user group, customer segment, tools, language, escalation rate)\n  ↓ (IRQ-GOV-003: approved conditions of use are the baseline this IRQ reconciles\n  actual use against)\nOne or more drift vectors occurs incrementally, each individually unremarkable:\n  • Business process drift — &quot;triage&quot; quietly becomes &quot;decision&quot; as escalation\n    review becomes a rubber stamp (IRQ-HUM-002: approval theatre)\n  • Prompt drift — business users edit system prompts toward permissiveness with\n    no re-test (IRQ-MOD-013: prompt change control)\n  • Tool/integration drift — the system is wired into downstream pricing, payment\n    or approval systems post-go-live, so its outputs now cascade into irreversible\n    actions never contemplated at approval (IRQ-AGT-010: workflow reconciliation)\n  • Customer-segment drift — the model, validated on prime retail, is applied to\n    self-employed or international applicants outside its tested population\n    (IRQ-MOD-001: capability envelope)\n  • Capability drift (silent) — a vendor-side model update expands capability\n    without the firm's knowledge (IRQ-TPR-004: vendor change detection)\n  ↓ (IRQ-GOV-012 — THIS IRQ: no periodic reconciliation of effective use against\n  approved envelope exists, or exists but does not trigger re-approval)\nNone of the original assurance evidence (validation, testing, risk appetite\nsign-off) covers the now-actual envelope; the safety case is stale\n  ↓ (IRQ-GOV-008: even if a monitoring signal exists somewhere for one drift\n  vector, it may not reach governance with envelope-drift framing)\nSystem operates, unremediated, under conditions materially different from those\nits assurance evidence was ever obtained for\n  ↓\nA failure mode never tested for the actual envelope materialises (e.g. hallucination\nrate untested for the self-employed segment; irreversible downstream action never\ngated because integration was never assessed) → customer harm → post-incident\nreview discovers the approved envelope had silently drifted months or years earlier\n→ finding that governance failed to detect its own approval basis had become false\n\nControl Breakpoints:\n  • IRQ-GOV-003: technically enforced, monitored conditions of use as the baseline\n    envelope statement\n  • IRQ-GOV-012: periodic, structured reconciliation of effective use against\n    approved envelope, with defined drift triggers for mandatory re-approval\n  • IRQ-MOD-001/-002: capability-envelope testing and re-approval on capability delta\n  • IRQ-AGT-010: per-execution workflow reconciliation feeding this IRQ's\n    population-level drift assessment\n  • IRQ-MOD-013: prompt/config version control as the primary defence against\n    silent prompt drift\n</code></pre>"},{"n":7,"title":"Why a Domain Expert May Miss It","html":"<ul>\n<li>Traditional model risk management validates a model <em>once</em> against a <em>static</em> specification; the idea that the thing requiring ongoing assurance is not the model but the evolving <em>relationship</em> between the model and an ever-changing business process, user population and toolset is foreign to that mental model — SMEs look for \"has the model changed?\" and, finding it unchanged, conclude no re-assurance is needed.</li>\n<li>Each individual drift vector, examined in isolation by the function that owns it, looks like an ordinary, low-risk operational change: a prompt tweak is a copy-editing task to the business user who makes it; a new downstream integration is a standard engineering change to the platform team; a shift in who reviews escalations is an ordinary staffing decision to the operations manager. No single function sees the <em>cumulative</em> divergence from the approved envelope, because no single function is tasked with looking at the envelope as a whole.</li>\n<li>The system's technical behaviour genuinely has not changed — the model output for a given input is the same as it was at approval — so any control that only asks \"does the model still perform as tested\" (a legitimate, necessary, but insufficient question) will pass, giving false reassurance that nothing requiring attention has occurred.</li>\n<li>Approval papers and safety cases are typically read once, at go-live, and rarely revisited as a living reconciliation target; without a deliberate mechanism to compare <em>today's</em> actual use against <em>that document</em>, drift is structurally invisible until an incident forces the comparison retrospectively.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":8,"title":"Assurance Objective","html":"<p><strong>AO-GOV-012:</strong> The system's effective, in-practice operating envelope — intended use, user population, prompt content, connected tools/integrations, and surrounding business process — shall be periodically and structurally reconciled against its approved operating envelope, with any material divergence (drift) triggering mandatory re-approval or re-assurance before the drifted use continues, regardless of whether the underlying model itself has changed.</p>"},{"n":9,"title":"Expected Controls (Traced)","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Explicit, documented Operating Envelope Declaration at approval — a yes-list/no-list of designed-for and not-designed-for scope (customer segments, languages, products, use case, escalation rate, stress scenarios) that serves as the reconciliation baseline</strong> — [ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md, Layer 4, AI Safety Case, \"Operating Envelope Declaration\"] — the FAIAS-native anchor for this control, itself grounded in the same safety-case discipline (ARP 4761; IEC 61508; ISO/IEC 15026-2) used in aviation and functional-safety domains for explicit, bounded scope-of-safety-claim declarations.</li>\n<li><strong>Periodic, structured reconciliation of effective use (actual task types, actual user population, current prompt/system-instruction content, current tool/integration map, current business-process description) against the approved envelope, at a defined cadence and on defined triggers (major change, incident, periodic review)</strong> — [PRA, SS1/23, May 2023, ongoing monitoring and periodic review principle, extended per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md's Periodic Assurance Phase] — the direct AI-neutral MRM anchor for \"does the model still operate as validated,\" extended here to the AI-specific question of whether <em>use</em> still matches the <em>validated</em> use.</li>\n<li><strong>Defined drift triggers mapped to specific vectors — business-process change, prompt/config change (linked IRQ-MOD-013), new tool/integration (linked IRQ-AGT-010), customer-segment expansion (linked IRQ-MOD-001), vendor-side silent capability change (linked IRQ-TPR-004) — each requiring mandatory re-approval before the drifted use continues</strong> — [EU AI Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 9 — continuous, iterative risk management system requiring the risk-management process to be regularly reviewed and updated throughout the lifecycle] — the clearest binding regulatory anchor requiring lifecycle-continuous (not point-in-time) risk management for in-scope high-risk systems, directly supporting a mandatory re-trigger requirement rather than a discretionary one.</li>\n<li><strong>Technical enforcement (not policy-only) of the approved envelope's boundaries where feasible — e.g. platform-level restriction of user groups, task types or connected tools to those approved, per IRQ-GOV-003 — reducing the population of drift vectors that depend on human process discipline alone</strong> — [linked IRQ-GOV-003; NIST AI 100-1, Jan 2023, GOVERN function] — narrows the surface this IRQ must monitor by preventing some drift vectors architecturally rather than only detecting them after the fact.</li>\n<li><strong>Named, empowered accountability for the reconciliation exercise and authority to require re-approval or suspend drifted use, distinct from (and escalating from) individual function owners who each control only one drift vector</strong> — [linked IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-HUM-005; ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md, Layer 2, Mandatory Completeness Rule] — closes the \"no single function sees the cumulative picture\" blind spot identified in field 7.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":10,"title":"Expected Evidence (Tagged L1–L5)","html":"<ul>\n<li>Assertion that \"the system operates as approved\" with no supporting reconciliation exercise — <strong>L1</strong> (near-worthless; this IRQ exists precisely because this assertion is what governance defaults to absent a structured check).</li>\n<li>Operating Envelope Declaration document (yes-list/no-list) produced at approval and version-controlled — <strong>L2</strong>.</li>\n<li>Reconciliation methodology and cadence documentation specifying what \"effective use\" evidence is gathered (actual task logs, actual user access logs, current prompt/system-instruction text, current integration map) and how it is compared against the declaration — <strong>L2</strong>.</li>\n<li>A completed reconciliation exercise for a specific system, with the actual-use evidence gathered and each drift vector explicitly assessed against the declaration, dated and retained — <strong>L3</strong>.</li>\n<li>Staging/configuration test attempting to exercise the system slightly outside its declared envelope (an out-of-scope task type, an unapproved user role, a downstream integration attempt) to test whether technical controls, not policy alone, resist the attempt — <strong>L3</strong>.</li>\n<li>Internal audit or independent review of the reconciliation control's design and operating effectiveness across a sample of systems, including whether any re-approval was actually triggered where drift was found — <strong>L4</strong>.</li>\n<li>Production evidence (last 12 months): actual task-type distribution, actual user access logs, prompt/config version history, and integration change log for a specific system, independently compared against its declared envelope, with any identified divergence and the governance action (or inaction) taken in response — <strong>L5</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":11,"title":"Independent Test","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>For a risk-based sample of approved AI systems, obtain the system's Operating Envelope Declaration (designed-for/not-designed-for scope) as recorded at approval. Independently gather current-state evidence across each drift vector: (1) a sample of actual production task/query logs, classified against the declared task-type scope; (2) current user-access records, checked against the declared user population; (3) the current system prompt/instruction text, diffed against the version in force at approval; (4) the current tool/integration map, checked for any connection not present at approval; (5) a structured interview with the business process owner on whether the surrounding human-review process (e.g. escalation review substance) still matches the process described at approval. For each drift vector, classify the finding as no drift, drift within tolerance (documented and approved), or undocumented drift. Separately, attempt in a staging replica to exercise the system slightly outside one declared boundary (e.g. submit an out-of-declared-scope task type, or attempt access as a non-approved user role) to test whether technical controls, not policy statement alone, resist the attempt. Cross-reference any undocumented drift found against whether a corresponding re-approval or re-assurance record exists. Methodology follows standard configuration/process-audit reconciliation practice (as-approved vs as-operating comparison, drawing on internal-audit walkthrough and sampling techniques and IT change-management drift-detection practice), extended to the AI-specific envelope-drift vector taxonomy defined in ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md Layer 4 (business-process, user-behaviour, tool/integration, prompt, customer-segment, and silent capability drift), for which no AI-specific benchmark or standardised detection methodology yet exists. Conducted against logs, configuration records, version-control history and interviews in a non-production/staging environment for the boundary-exercise component; no live production system access required for the reconciliation component beyond retrieving historical logs. — <strong>★2 Confidence — as at FAIAS v1.1.0 (25 August 2026) · P1 (§6A.4)</strong> (the underlying reconciliation technique — as-approved vs as-operating comparison — is established, credible audit practice, but the specific six-vector envelope-drift taxonomy this test is built around is FAIAS/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE-native rather than an externally published, benchmarked methodology; no cross-institution consensus exists yet on what constitutes a complete drift-vector checklist or an acceptable drift-detection sampling frame, so test design and the materiality threshold for \"undocumented drift\" rest heavily on reviewer professional judgement — this is stated honestly rather than inflated, consistent with this being an assurance-architecture-native rather than externally standardised test).</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":12,"title":"Pass Indicators","html":"<ul>\n<li>Actual task/query distribution for the sampled systems falls entirely within the declared task-type scope, with any exceptions documented and separately re-approved.</li>\n<li>Current user-access records match the declared user population; no access group exists in production that was not in the approved declaration.</li>\n<li>Current system-prompt text matches the version in force at approval, or any change is version-controlled with an accompanying re-test record.</li>\n<li>No tool/integration exists in production that was not present, or explicitly approved as an addition with re-assessment, at the time of original approval.</li>\n<li>The staging boundary-exercise attempt is technically resisted (not merely discouraged by policy) for at least the tested boundary.</li>\n<li>Where any drift is found, a corresponding re-approval or re-assurance record exists, dated at or before the drift's introduction.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":13,"title":"Fail Indicators","html":"<ul>\n<li>Production task/query logs show a material proportion of activity outside the declared task-type scope, with no corresponding re-approval record (the clearest and most common finding: \"triage\" tool functioning as a \"decision\" tool).</li>\n<li>Current system-prompt text differs materially from the approved-at-go-live version with no version-control trail and no re-test evidence.</li>\n<li>A downstream integration or tool connection exists in production that was not present or contemplated at approval, and no re-assessment of the consequent risk (e.g. irreversible downstream action) has occurred.</li>\n<li>The staging boundary-exercise attempt succeeds — the system accepts an out-of-declared-scope task or an unapproved user role with no technical resistance, confirming envelope boundaries exist only in policy documentation.</li>\n<li>Business-process-owner interview reveals the surrounding human-review process has materially changed (e.g. escalation review has become nominal) since approval, with no corresponding reassessment of the system's effective autonomy.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":14,"title":"Reporting Language","html":"<p><strong>Pass:</strong> \"Reconciliation of effective use against the approved Operating Envelope Declaration for [n] sampled systems found no undocumented drift across the tested vectors (task scope, user population, prompt content, tool/integration map, surrounding process); the staging boundary-exercise confirmed technical, not merely policy-based, enforcement of at least the tested boundary. Conclusions rest on ★2 methodology — a FAIAS/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE-native drift-vector taxonomy applied via established audit-reconciliation technique, with no external benchmark for envelope-drift detection — and should be read as professional assessment, re-performed at the next periodic review or major-change trigger.\"\n<strong>Fail:</strong> \"Reconciliation testing found that [system] is now operating materially outside its approved Operating Envelope Declaration: [specific finding, e.g. the escalation-review process approved at 10% human sign-off now functions as a rubric-only pass-through; the system has been integrated into a downstream pricing engine never assessed at approval]. No re-approval or re-assurance record exists for this drift. Because the original validation evidence, testing and risk-appetite sign-off were obtained against the approved envelope, none of that assurance basis demonstrably covers the system's actual current operation. This is a governance-integrity failure independent of whether the underlying model itself has changed, and should trigger immediate re-assurance before further reliance is placed on the existing approval.\"\n<strong>Partial:</strong> \"Reconciliation confirms task scope, user population and tool/integration map remain within the approved envelope, but the current system-prompt text has diverged from the approved-at-go-live version with no version-control trail identified, preventing confirmation that prompt-level drift has not occurred. The assurance objective is not yet met for the prompt-drift vector specifically; remediation should establish prompt version control and confirm or remediate drift within [n] days.\"</p>"},{"n":15,"title":"Residual Judgement","html":"<pre><code>Assuming controls are well-implemented and monitored:\n  Severity: 3 (drift converts a system whose assurance basis was calibrated to a\n              bounded, tested envelope into one operating, untested, against an\n              expanded envelope; realised severity depends on the specific drift\n              vector but commonly involves reduced human oversight or expanded\n              customer-segment exposure, both individually rated 3-4 in their own\n              home domains)\n  Likelihood: 3 (foreseeable and, per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md's own six named\n              drift vectors, probable over any extended deployment lifetime absent\n              structured periodic reconciliation — prompt and business-process drift\n              in particular require no formal change-control trigger at all, and\n              therefore occur by default rather than by exception)\n  Detectability: 4 (structurally invisible to any control that only asks &quot;has the\n              model changed&quot; — precisely the question most existing MRM/governance\n              processes already ask — because the model, by this IRQ's own\n              definition, has not; only a dedicated envelope-reconciliation exercise\n              surfaces it)\n  Recoverability: 2 (once identified, re-approval or restriction of the drifted use\n              is a governance-process action achievable within a defined period, but\n              any customer decisions made during the undetected-drift window under\n              an effectively untested envelope cannot be retrospectively re-assured)\n  Novelty: 4 (this is precisely the risk the Novelty axis anchors describe at its\n              highest level: emergent, outside the traditional banking risk model,\n              and very likely to be missed by a domain expert whose entire mental\n              model of &quot;has this system changed&quot; is keyed to model version, not to\n              the surrounding use pattern — this is also why ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md\n              names envelope drift as a distinct, structural concept rather than\n              folding it into ordinary change management)\n\nInterpretation: this is the flagship governance risk in the FAIAS domain set —\nhigh severity, foreseeable likelihood absent structural controls, and the single\nworst detectability profile of any GOV IRQ, because the standard question every\nother governance and MRM control asks (&quot;has the model changed?&quot;) returns a\nreassuring &quot;no&quot; even while the actual risk picture has moved substantially.\nStructured periodic reconciliation against an explicit Operating Envelope\nDeclaration is not an optional refinement; it is the only control that asks the\nright question.\n\nIf controls are absent or degraded:\n  Severity: 4 (systemic: envelope drift is rarely a single-system event — the same\n              organisational pressures, e.g. &quot;let's use the triage tool for\n              decisions too, it's working well,&quot; recur across a deployment estate;\n              undetected, compounding drift across multiple systems is plausible\n              and can produce widespread customer harm operating under an\n              assurance basis that no longer describes reality)\n  Likelihood: 4 (near-certain over any multi-year deployment lifetime with no\n              structural reconciliation mechanism, given prompt and business-process\n              drift require no formal trigger to occur)\n  Detectability: 4 (silent by construction; discovered only via a dedicated\n              reconciliation exercise or, worse, via a post-incident investigation\n              that asks &quot;wait, is this what we actually approved?&quot;)\n  Recoverability: 3 (the drift itself is correctable through re-approval, but the\n              specific decisions and outcomes produced during the undetected-drift\n              period, potentially spanning years, cannot be retrospectively\n              re-assured or, in many cases, even fully reconstructed for remediation)\n  Novelty: 4\nResidual risk: Critical if no structural reconciliation mechanism exists. This IRQ\nshould be treated as a standing deployment-gate condition (periodic reconciliation\ncadence defined and evidenced) for any system with a multi-year expected lifetime,\nconsistent with ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md's Periodic Assurance and Major Change\nReview phases.\n</code></pre>"},{"n":16,"title":"Risk Weighting Profile (5 Axes, 1–4)","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Axis</th>\n<th>Rating</th>\n<th>Justification</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Severity</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Drift converts a system with a bounded, tested assurance basis into one operating, untested, against an expanded envelope; realised harm is commonly reduced human oversight or expanded customer-segment exposure, each individually severe.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Likelihood</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Foreseeable and probable over any extended deployment lifetime absent structural reconciliation; prompt and business-process drift require no formal change-control trigger and so occur by default.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Detectability</strong></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Invisible to any control that only asks \"has the model changed\" — the question most existing MRM processes already ask — because by this IRQ's own definition the model has not changed; only a dedicated reconciliation exercise surfaces it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Recoverability</strong></td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Re-approval or restriction is achievable once identified, but decisions made during the undetected-drift window under an effectively untested envelope cannot be retrospectively re-assured.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Novelty</strong></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The archetypal AI-native governance blind spot: outside the traditional banking risk model, emergent, and very likely to be missed by a domain expert whose \"has this changed\" instinct is keyed to model version rather than surrounding use pattern.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"n":17,"title":"Regulatory Basis","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>[EU AI Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 9] requires a continuous, iterative risk-management system, regularly reviewed and updated throughout the AI system's lifecycle — the clearest binding requirement that risk management (and, by extension, the scope it was performed against) cannot be a point-in-time exercise, directly supporting a mandatory re-trigger requirement for envelope drift in Annex III high-risk systems. [PRA, SS1/23, May 2023] ongoing monitoring and periodic review principle requires firms to confirm models continue to perform and be used as validated, extended here to the AI-specific question of whether <em>use</em> still matches <em>validated use</em>, not only whether the model's technical performance is stable. [Bank of England/PRA, \"PRA response to Government on AI regulation,\" Apr 2024] and the PRA's Oct 2025 MRM AI/ML roundtable both signal increasing supervisory attention to how AI use evolves post-approval. No UK/FS regulation names \"operating envelope drift\" as a defined term; this IRQ's framing is FAIAS's own structural contribution (per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md, Layer 4), built on the continuous-risk-management obligations cited above. Section pinpoints [UNVERIFIED — cited modules/articles confirmed extant (PRA SS1/23, SS1/21, PS21/3, ISO/IEC 42001:2023, EU AI Act Arts. 9/11/12/19); no AI-specific sub-clause pinpoint published by the regulator beyond module/clause level cited] (verified 29 Jul 2026).</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":18,"title":"Research Basis","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>The envelope-drift concept and its named drift vectors (business-process, user-behaviour, tool/integration, prompt, customer-segment, silent capability drift) are drawn directly from ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md's Layer 4 AI Safety Case framework, itself grounded in the safety-case discipline of aviation and functional-safety standards [ARP 4761, SAE International; IEC 61508; ISO/IEC 15026-2, Systems and Software Assurance — Assurance Case] where an explicit, bounded \"operating envelope\" or \"operational design domain\" and its drift are long-established structural concepts (the closest direct analogue being the automated-driving-systems Operational Design Domain concept in UL 4600 and ISO 34503). No FS-specific academic literature yet quantifies AI operating-envelope drift as a distinct empirical phenomenon; this is consistent with the concept's status as an emerging, cross-domain-transferred safety-engineering construct rather than a benchmarked AI-safety research finding, and is stated honestly as such (★2, not ★3+, for the Independent Test above).</p>\n<p><strong>Research basis last reviewed:</strong> 1 August 2026 · <strong>review cadence:</strong> Standard (12 months)</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":19,"title":"Related IRQs","html":"<pre><code>  - IRQ-GOV-003 (I.2: technically enforced conditions of use — the approved-envelope\n    baseline this IRQ's reconciliation is performed against)\n  - IRQ-MOD-001 (B.1: capability envelope testing — the model-capability-boundary\n    dimension of drift; distinguished from this IRQ's population-level use-profile\n    drift per TAXONOMY.md ruling 14)\n  - IRQ-AGT-010 (C.7: per-execution workflow reconciliation — supplies the\n    within-run divergence signal that, aggregated over time, feeds this IRQ's\n    population-level drift assessment; distinguished per TAXONOMY.md ruling 14)\n  - IRQ-MOD-013 (B.6: prompt/config version control — the primary technical defence\n    against the prompt-drift vector)\n  - IRQ-TPR-004 (F.2: vendor model-change detection — the primary defence against\n    the silent capability-drift vector)\n  - IRQ-HUM-002 (D.1: approval-theatre detection — the mechanism by which\n    business-process drift, specifically escalation review becoming nominal, is\n    independently evidenced)\n  - IRQ-GOV-008 (I.3: monitoring signals reaching governance — envelope-drift\n    findings depend on this IRQ's escalation route to actually trigger re-approval)\n</code></pre>"},{"n":20,"title":"References","html":"<pre><code>[1] European Union, 2024. &quot;Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 — Artificial Intelligence Act.&quot;\n    OJ L 12.7.2024. Art. 9, Risk Management System.\n[2] PRA, 2023. &quot;SS1/23 — Model risk management principles for banks.&quot; May 2023, effective\n    17 May 2024.\n[3] Bank of England/PRA, 2024. &quot;PRA response to Government on AI regulation.&quot; April 2024.\n[4] Bank of England/PRA, 2025. &quot;PRA model risk management roundtable on AI and ML\n    technologies.&quot; Meeting note, published November 2025.\n[5] SAE International, 2010. &quot;ARP 4761 — Guidelines and Methods for Conducting the Safety\n    Assessment Process on Civil Airborne Systems and Equipment.&quot;\n[6] IEC, 2010. &quot;IEC 61508 — Functional Safety of Electrical/Electronic/Programmable\n    Electronic Safety-Related Systems.&quot;\n[7] ISO/IEC, 2011. &quot;ISO/IEC 15026-2 — Systems and Software Assurance — Part 2: Assurance\n    Case.&quot;\n[8] Underwriters Laboratories, 2023. &quot;UL 4600 — Standard for Safety for the Evaluation of\n    Autonomous Products.&quot; (Operational Design Domain concept, cited as cross-domain\n    analogue.)\n</code></pre>"},{"n":21,"title":"Four-Test Validation Footer","html":"<pre><code>---\nVersion: the release version in README.md\nSubdomain: I.2 (Governance domain, Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy) — Flagship IRQ\nStatus: Active\n\nFOUR-TEST VALIDATION:\n✓ Test 1 (SME Blind Spot): The archetypal blind spot this IRQ exists to close: every\n  traditional model-governance instinct asks &quot;has the model changed?&quot;, and envelope drift\n  by definition occurs while the answer remains &quot;no&quot; — the model is unchanged; only the use\n  made of it has expanded, invisibly to that question.\n✓ Test 2 (Plausible Harm): Grounded in ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md's Layer 4 Safety Case\n  concept (Operating Envelope Declaration, Envelope Drift as Named Concept) and the EU AI\n  Act Art. 9 continuous risk-management obligation; harm scenarios (triage-to-decision\n  drift, silent tool integration, prompt permissiveness creep) are drawn directly from the\n  named drift vectors in that framework.\n✓ Test 3 (Testable): Testable via structured reconciliation of current-state evidence\n  (task logs, access logs, prompt version diff, integration map, process-owner interview)\n  against the approved Operating Envelope Declaration, plus a staging boundary-exercise\n  test of technical enforcement. ★2 Confidence — honestly rated: the reconciliation\n  technique is established audit practice, but the six-vector drift taxonomy is\n  FAIAS-native with no external benchmark, and this is stated rather than inflated.\n✓ Test 4 (Decision-Relevant): Directly gates whether the firm's existing approval, safety\n  case and risk-appetite sign-off can still be relied upon at all — a &quot;yes&quot; finding on drift\n  invalidates reliance on every other IRQ's assurance conclusions for the affected system\n  until re-assurance occurs. This is the flagship IRQ of the Governance domain.\n\nChange Log:\n- v1.0 (2026-07): Initial publication; all four tests passed as the domain's flagship IRQ.\n- v1.3.2 (2026-10-03): Corrections listed in 00_Admin/CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-03.md §8; no change to the ★ rating or the risk profile.\n</code></pre>\n<h2>---</h2>"}],"procedure":"<h2>1. Procedure Identity</h2>\n<p><strong>Procedure ID:</strong> TP-GOV-012\n<strong>IRQ:</strong> IRQ-GOV-012 — Effective Operating Envelope Has Drifted from the Approved Envelope Without Re-Assurance\n<strong>Assurance objective:</strong> AO-GOV-012 — The system's effective, in-practice operating envelope — intended use, user population, prompt content, connected tools/integrations, and surrounding business process — shall be periodically and structurally reconciled against its approved operating envelope, with any material divergence triggering mandatory re-approval or re-assurance before the drifted use continues, regardless of whether the underlying model itself has changed.\n<strong>★ Confidence (carried from canvas, unchanged):</strong> ★2 — the underlying reconciliation technique (as-approved vs as-operating comparison) is established, credible audit practice, but the specific six-vector envelope-drift taxonomy this test is built around is FAIAS/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE-native rather than an externally published, benchmarked methodology; no cross-institution consensus exists on what constitutes a complete drift-vector checklist or an acceptable drift-detection sampling frame, so test design and the materiality threshold for \"undocumented drift\" rest heavily on reviewer professional judgement.\n<strong>Method classification:</strong> Adapted Industry Practice (configuration/process-audit reconciliation, IT change-management drift detection) applied through an <strong>Emerging Practice</strong> drift-vector taxonomy that is FAIAS-native. This must be stated in every report using this procedure.</p>\n<h2>2. Preconditions</h2>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> Documentary and log reconciliation, plus a staging replica for the boundary-exercise component. No live production access is required for the reconciliation limb beyond retrieving historical logs; the boundary exercise runs in staging only.\n<strong>Access required:</strong> The Operating Envelope Declaration recorded at approval; production task/query logs (metadata level wherever sufficient); current user-access records; current system prompt/instruction text and its version history; the current tool/integration map; the business process owner for interview; a staging replica for the boundary exercise.\n<strong>Artefacts required:</strong> Operating Envelope Declaration (designed-for / not-designed-for scope) per sampled system; approval papers; task/query logs for the review period; access records; system prompt version history; tool/integration inventory at approval and now; re-approval and re-assurance records; the process description recorded at approval.\n<strong>Personnel required:</strong> System owner; platform administrator; the business process owner (for the human-review-substance vector, which no log can answer); reviewer.\n<strong>Data handling:</strong> Task/query logs are the most sensitive artefact in this procedure. Request classification-level metadata (task type, timestamp, user role) rather than prompt content wherever the classification can be performed without it. Where content inspection is needed to classify task type, sample narrowly, review in situ under supervision, do not export, and record the minimisation approach.</p>\n<h2>3. Test Design</h2>\n<p>This is the flagship GOV procedure: the effective-use versus approved-envelope comparison. It exists because a system can drift into a materially different risk profile without a single change to the model — and every other assurance control in the firm is triggered by model change.</p>\n<p>Six drift vectors, per ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md Layer 4. Each is assessed independently and classified as <strong>no drift</strong>, <strong>drift within tolerance</strong> (documented and approved), or <strong>undocumented drift</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V1 Business-process drift</strong> — the surrounding process has changed; e.g. escalation review has become nominal.</li>\n<li><strong>V2 User-behaviour drift</strong> — actual task types submitted diverge from the declared scope; the canonical case is a \"triage\" tool functioning as a \"decision\" tool.</li>\n<li><strong>V3 Tool/integration drift</strong> — connections exist that were not present or contemplated at approval.</li>\n<li><strong>V4 Prompt drift</strong> — the system prompt has changed materially since approval.</li>\n<li><strong>V5 Customer-segment drift</strong> — the population served has expanded beyond the declared segment.</li>\n<li><strong>V6 Silent capability drift</strong> — the underlying model or platform has gained capability without a firm-initiated change (cross-reference IRQ-TPR-004 and IRQ-MOD-002).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each vector has its own evidence source: V1 interview, V2 logs, V3 integration map, V4 prompt diff, V5 access and segment records, V6 provider release notes and version pinning records.</p>\n<p>A staging boundary exercise then tests whether the declared boundaries are technically resisted or exist only in policy — the same logic as TP-GOV-003 Limb C, applied to the envelope rather than to approval conditions.</p>\n<p>Finally, every instance of undocumented drift is cross-referenced against whether a re-approval or re-assurance record exists, dated at or before the drift's introduction.</p>\n<p>Sample: risk-based, weighted to Tier 2–3, minimum six systems, with preference for systems approved 12+ months ago (drift needs time to accumulate) — a reviewer-set design minimum, disclosed at scoping.</p>\n<h2>4. Steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw and record the sample, preferring systems approved 12+ months ago. <strong>Record:</strong> basis, size, approval-age spread.</li>\n<li>For each system, obtain the Operating Envelope Declaration as recorded at approval. <strong>Record:</strong> the declared task-type scope; the declared user population; the declared customer segment; the tool/integration set at approval; the system prompt version in force at approval; the surrounding process as described. Where no Envelope Declaration exists, record that — and note that TP-GOV-009 question 4 predicts this.</li>\n<li><strong>V2 — user-behaviour drift.</strong> Obtain task/query logs for the review period and classify a sample against the declared task-type scope. <strong>Record:</strong> sample size and classification method; the proportion of activity outside declared scope; specifically whether a declared \"triage/support\" tool is being used to make decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>V5 — customer-segment drift.</strong> Obtain current user-access records and, where applicable, the customer segments actually served. <strong>Record:</strong> any access group or customer segment present now and absent from the declaration.</li>\n<li><strong>V4 — prompt drift.</strong> Obtain the current system prompt/instruction text and diff it against the version in force at approval. <strong>Record:</strong> the diff; whether changes are material to behaviour; whether each change is version-controlled with an accompanying re-test record.</li>\n<li><strong>V3 — tool/integration drift.</strong> Obtain the current tool/integration map and compare to the approval-time set. <strong>Record:</strong> every connection present now and absent at approval; for each, whether it enables an irreversible downstream action; whether any re-assessment occurred.</li>\n<li><strong>V6 — silent capability drift.</strong> Review provider release notes and the firm's version pinning records for the period. <strong>Record:</strong> any capability the model or platform gained without a firm-initiated change; whether the firm detected it. Cross-reference IRQ-TPR-004.</li>\n<li><strong>V1 — business-process drift.</strong> Interview the business process owner structurally: is the human-review step performed as described at approval; has review time per case changed; has the reviewer population changed; is escalation still substantive. <strong>Record:</strong> verbatim; specifically any account of review having become nominal.</li>\n<li>Classify each vector per system as no drift / drift within tolerance / undocumented drift. <strong>Record:</strong> the classification matrix — six vectors by however many systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Boundary exercise.</strong> In the staging replica, attempt to exercise the system slightly outside one declared boundary: submit an out-of-declared-scope task type, and attempt access as a non-approved user role. Use benign test content only. <strong>Record:</strong> blocked / flagged / silently permitted; verbatim system response.</li>\n<li>For every undocumented drift found, search for a corresponding re-approval or re-assurance record. <strong>Record:</strong> whether one exists and its date relative to the drift's introduction. A record dated after the drift began is not re-assurance; it is retrospective documentation.</li>\n<li>Assess aggregate effect. <strong>Record:</strong> the reviewer's judgement on whether the accumulated drift across vectors has materially changed the system's risk profile relative to the tier assigned at approval — cross-reference TP-GOV-004.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5. Observations &amp; Measures</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Threshold</th>\n<th>Basis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Task/query activity outside declared scope</td>\n<td>Reviewer judgement on materiality — no established threshold. The canvas treats a material proportion with no re-approval as the clearest and most common fail indicator</td>\n<td>Explicitly bespoke; no consensus exists on an acceptable drift-detection sampling frame</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User-access records vs declared population</td>\n<td>No access group in production absent from the declaration</td>\n<td>Categorical per canvas pass indicator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System prompt diff</td>\n<td>Matches approval version, or every change version-controlled with a re-test record</td>\n<td>Categorical per canvas pass indicator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tool/integration additions</td>\n<td>None absent from approval without explicit approved addition and re-assessment</td>\n<td>Categorical per canvas pass indicator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boundary exercise outcome</td>\n<td>Technically resisted, not merely discouraged by policy</td>\n<td>Categorical per canvas pass indicator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Re-approval record dated at or before drift introduction</td>\n<td>Required wherever drift is found</td>\n<td>Categorical per canvas pass indicator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Completeness of the six-vector taxonomy</td>\n<td>Reviewer judgement — no established threshold. The taxonomy is FAIAS/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE-native and not externally benchmarked; this must be disclosed</td>\n<td>Explicitly bespoke; the primary reason for ★2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>6. Interpretation (per CANVASES_GOV.md fields 12–14)</h2>\n<p><strong>Pass</strong> where: actual task/query distribution falls entirely within the declared task-type scope with any exceptions documented and separately re-approved; user-access records match the declared population; current system-prompt text matches the approved version or every change is version-controlled with a re-test record; no tool/integration exists in production that was not present or explicitly approved as an addition with re-assessment; the staging boundary attempt is technically resisted; and any drift found has a corresponding re-approval or re-assurance record dated at or before its introduction.</p>\n<p><strong>Fail</strong> where: task/query logs show a material proportion of activity outside declared scope with no re-approval (the \"triage tool functioning as a decision tool\" pattern); system-prompt text differs materially from the approved version with no version-control trail and no re-test evidence; a downstream integration or tool connection exists that was not contemplated at approval with no re-assessment of consequent risk (particularly irreversible downstream action); the staging boundary attempt succeeds with no technical resistance, confirming envelope boundaries exist only in policy; or the business-process-owner interview reveals the human-review process has materially changed — escalation review become nominal — with no reassessment of the system's effective autonomy.</p>\n<p><strong>Partial</strong> where some vectors show no drift and others show undocumented drift. Given six vectors, Partial is the expected outcome for most real systems; report per vector rather than as a single verdict, and state which vectors were tested and which could not be (e.g. V6 where provider release notes are unavailable).</p>\n<h2>7. Evidence Outputs</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Operating Envelope Declaration and approval papers as obtained — <strong>L2</strong></li>\n<li>Tool/integration map at approval and now; prompt version history — <strong>L2</strong></li>\n<li>Task/query log classification results (V2) — <strong>L3</strong></li>\n<li>Prompt diff (V4); integration comparison (V3); access record comparison (V5) — <strong>L3</strong></li>\n<li>Business process owner interview transcript (V1) — <strong>L3</strong></li>\n<li>Staging boundary-exercise log with verbatim responses — <strong>L3</strong></li>\n<li>Reviewer's independent six-vector drift classification matrix and aggregate risk-profile assessment — <strong>L4</strong></li>\n<li>Re-approval/re-assurance records and any real historical drift detection by the firm's own mechanism — <strong>L5</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>8. Common Pitfalls &amp; Limitations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No Operating Envelope Declaration to reconcile against.</strong> The most common blocker. Where none exists, the procedure cannot produce a drift finding — it produces a more fundamental finding (no envelope was ever declared) and must be reported that way rather than as a Pass. Sequence TP-GOV-009 first; its question 4 predicts this.</li>\n<li><strong>Classifying task types from prompt content at scale.</strong> Expensive and data-sensitive. Where an automated classifier is used, validate it on a manually-labelled subset and report its accuracy; an unvalidated classifier makes the headline drift proportion unreliable.</li>\n<li><strong>V1 has no log.</strong> Business-process drift is invisible in every technical artefact and only surfaces in the interview. It is also the vector with the largest effect on effective autonomy. Do not skip it because it is unquantifiable.</li>\n<li><strong>V6 depends on the provider.</strong> Where release notes are absent or the model is not version-pinned, V6 cannot be assessed; record the limitation rather than recording no drift.</li>\n<li><strong>Retrospective re-approval.</strong> A re-approval paper dated after the drift began documents the drift; it does not re-assure it. Step 11's date comparison is the discriminating check.</li>\n<li><strong>Aggregate versus vector-level findings.</strong> Six individually tolerable drifts can compound into a materially different system. Step 12 exists for this and is where the procedure earns its flagship status.</li>\n<li><strong>The taxonomy is FAIAS-native.</strong> This is the single most important disclosure in the GOV domain. The six-vector set is not an externally published or cross-institution-agreed checklist; another reviewer might define a seventh vector or merge two. Every report using TP-GOV-012 must carry that statement alongside the ★2 rating, honestly, rather than presenting the taxonomy as settled methodology.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>9. Time / Effort Estimate</h2>\n<p><strong>6 reviewer-days</strong>: 0.5 day sampling and envelope extraction, 1.5 days task/query log classification (V2, the heaviest limb), 1 day prompt/integration/access comparison (V3, V4, V5), 0.5 day silent-capability review (V6), 0.5 day business process interviews (V1), 0.5 day staging boundary exercises, 1.5 days classification matrix, aggregate assessment and working papers. Add 1 day per system beyond six; add 2 days where an automated task-type classifier must be built and validated.</p>\n<h2>---</h2>"};
